<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at the Market.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>
            Mediation: Text: Interpret short, simple messages from class texts.<br>
            Communication: Assist peers in following oral directions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>
            Mediation: Can interpret short, simple messages about locations using gestures (e.g., points to "hospital" when hearing "I'm at the hospital.").<br>
            Can assist peers in following directions by pointing or using gestures (e.g., points to "home" on a map).<br>
            Can guide peers by demonstrating simple actions to show locations (e.g., pointing to "park" and saying "This is the park.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of key vocabulary and grammar for the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of familiar places (market, school, home, park, hospital) around the classroom. Students stand up. The teacher calls out a place, e.g., "Market!" Students point to the flashcard and say, "I'm at the market!" The activity continues for several places. This activates prior knowledge and reviews vocabulary for locations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target phrase "I'm at the [place]." clearly, pointing to a flashcard. "I'm at the school." "I'm at the market." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /sk/ in "school" and /m/ in "market". Teacher demonstrates using gestures to show location, e.g., pointing ahead for "park" or to a specific corner for "store".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a library) "Is this a park?" (No.) "What is this place?" (Library!) "Yes, it's a library. So, I'm at the...?" (Library!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing two flashcards: 'home' and 'school') "If I say 'I'm at home', do I point here (school) or here (home)?" (Home!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated dialogue on a projected slide or large poster, depicting two friends talking about where they are or where they want to go. The dialogue includes places like "market," "park," "school," and uses "I'm at the..." and "Are you at the...?" phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Boy: "Hi, Ana! Where are you?"<br>
                Girl: "Hi, Leo! I'm at the market. It's busy here!"<br>
                Boy: "Oh, really? I'm at the park. It's quiet."<br>
                Girl: "Are you near the big slide?"<br>
                Boy: "Yes, I'm near the slide. Come play later!"<br>
                Girl: "Okay! Thank you! See you later!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing locations: "I'm at school." "I'm at home." Confirming locations: "Are you at school?" "Yes, I'm at school." Pragmatic use of polite expressions for responding to compliments or ending a conversation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "Where is Ana? Where is Leo?" (Students point to pictures or say the names of the places).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "Leo is at the market. True or False?" (False). "Ana is at the park. True or False?" (False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match a character's name to the place they are, using lines or drawing a connection.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation. Students practice using the language to help others understand information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Contractions ("I'm"), Prepositions of place ("at home", "near the slide"), Present simple ("I'm at school."). Vocabulary: home, school, market, park, library, store, hospital, pharmacy, bakery, restaurant. Pronunciation: /sk/ and /m/ sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Location Match & Explain (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with pictures of different places (e.g., hospital, market, school) and scrambled phrases (e.g., "at hospital I'm the", "market the at I'm"). Students match the picture to the correct, unscrambled sentence. After matching, in pairs, one student points to a picture and the other reads the sentence, clearly explaining "This is the hospital. I'm at the hospital." using gestures if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Where Am I?" Drawing Game (Semi-controlled Practice for Mediation):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student secretly draws a simple map with 3-4 key places (market, park, home, school). Student A then describes their location on their map, e.g., "I'm at the market. It's busy. My home is near the market." Student B listens, interprets, and points to where they think Student A is on *their own* map, or on a shared blank map. Student A confirms by saying "Yes, you're at the market!" or "No, I'm not here. My market is big, not small." This requires students to mediate their drawings and descriptions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Map Guide (Pair Interaction & Peer Check for Mediation):</b> In pairs, students use a simple classroom map or a set of flashcards arranged to form a "town." One student is the "Guide" and the other is the "Explorer." The Guide has a list of simple directions (e.g., "Go to the bakery.", "Then, walk to the library.", "Next, see the hospital."). The Guide reads the directions. The Explorer listens, interprets the directions, and uses their finger to trace the path on the map/flashcards, pointing to each location. The Guide provides feedback and clarification, helping the Explorer understand, e.g., "Yes, this is the bakery! Good job!" or "Excuse me, not there. The hospital is next to the school."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on the 21st Century Skills Project: Digital Map Tour.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group presentation and demonstration of a section of their "Digital Map Tour" project (or a paper prototype if digital tools are limited). Students will work in small groups (2-3 students) to present their map to another group or to the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each small group presents a segment of their "Digital Map Tour" of familiar places. They show their chosen places (e.g., "my home," "the market," "the park," "the school") on their map, labeled with "I'm at the [place]."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">As they present, students take turns pointing to locations on their map and verbally explaining what they might do there, using "I'm at the [place] to [verb]." For example, "I'm at the market to buy food," "I'm at the park to play."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The key mediation aspect is explaining their map to the audience (another group or the teacher). They must interpret their own labels and guide peers, using gestures and clear language to ensure understanding. For instance, if a peer asks, "Where is the library?", the presenting group must point and say, "The library is here, near the school. I'm at the library to read books." They use polite expressions like "Excuse me" or "Thank you" if clarifying or receiving feedback.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups will also assist their audience in following a simple oral direction on their map, e.g., "Find the bakery on our map."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' ability to mediate information and use target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentations and peer assessment during map interpretation activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present a segment of their "Digital Map Tour" to another group or the teacher. They must interpret their map labels and guide peers through a short "tour" on their map.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Guidance:</b> Can the student clearly explain locations on their map to peers using gestures? (Learning Outcome: Can guide peers by demonstrating simple actions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interpretation:</b> Can the student interpret short, simple messages (labels) from their map? (Specific Objective: Interpret short, simple messages).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assistance:</b> Does the student assist peers in following oral directions (e.g., "Find the hospital") by pointing or clarifying? (Learning Outcome: Can assist peers in following directions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Language Use:</b> Correct use of "I'm at the [place]", prepositions of place (at, near), and target vocabulary (market, school, home, park, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of /sk/ (school) and /m/ (market).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Works effectively with group members and engages with the audience.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult when showing your map and explaining it to your friends?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new place can you talk about now?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and look ahead.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What did you learn today about helping your friends understand places on a map?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which polite expression did you use today, and when?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mediation activities (explaining the map, guiding peers) were effective because students actively used gestures and verbal cues to clarify information for others. The "Where Am I?" game was particularly good for developing interpretive skills.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Some students still struggle with distinguishing between "at the" and "in the." More explicit practice with picture examples will be needed. Providing sentence frames for guiding descriptions (e.g., "This is the ____. It is next to the ____.") would provide more scaffolding for 3rd graders.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson could build on describing places by focusing on activities done in these locations, or expanding to describing one's daily routine involving these places, linking to present simple actions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>